      ],
      timeline: [
        '1940s: Game originated in South America',
        'Mid-20th century: Spread throughout Brazil and Argentina',
        '1990s: Italian variation Burraco became extremely popular in Italy',
        '2000s-present: International tournaments and online play established',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game family has three main branches: Brazilian Buraco (also called Canastra, Biriba, Perida), Italian Burraco (with significant rule differences), and Argentinean Burako (played with tiles instead of cards). Each variant has developed unique rules while maintaining core mechanics.',
      cultural: "Buraco is one of the most popular card games in Brazil and Italy. In Brazil, it's commonly played in social clubs, bars, and family gatherings. Italian Burraco has competitive leagues and tournaments. The game bridges social classes and generations in both countries.",
    },
